✅ Problem Statement
Given: A number n
Goal: Count how many numbers with unique digits exist, where the number has up to n digits.

👉 We count all numbers with 1-digit, 2-digit, ..., up to n-digit that have no repeated digits.

🔍 Example
Input:
n = 2
Output:
91
Explanation:
Total numbers with:
1-digit: 10 (0 to 9)
2-digit: 9×9 = 81 (first digit: 1–9, second digit: any digit except the first)

➡️ Total = 10 + 81 = 91

💡 Key Idea
We count the number of valid numbers for each digit length from 1 to n, ensuring digits are unique.
For 1-digit → 10 choices (0 to 9)
For 2-digit:
First digit: 1 to 9 (9 options — can’t be 0)
Second digit: 9 remaining options (excluding the one already used)
For 3-digit:
First: 1 to 9 (9 options)
Second: 9 options
Third: 8 options
And so on...
So for each i (number of digits), we use:
count = 9 × (9) × (8) × ... for (i-1) digits

